				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "bdce1511-722d-4596-9447-f1f813b7de44")
	)
	(text "The circuit consists of two parts: the power source and the LED flasher.\n\nPower source:\nIn a bright environment, the solar cell produces a voltage, and if it is\nhigher than the voltage in the storage capacitor, a current can flow\nfrom the solar cell into the storage capacitor. In darkness, the flasher\ncircuit operates on the voltage available in the storage capacitor.\nThe diode prevents current from \"flowing back\" through the solar cell when\nthe capacitor is charged but the voltage at the solar cell is\nlow in the dark.\n\nSolar cell:\nANYSOLAR makes beautiful, small solar cells, especially the\nIXOLAR SolarBIT (KXOB*) and SolarMD (SM*) series.\nWe use a cell with 5.53V nominal open-circuit voltage. Our experiments\nshowed that the voltage after the diode at the main capacitor doesn't\nexceed the maximum 3.8V that our capacitor allows.\n\nStorage capacitor:\nWe use an EDLC (Electrochemical Double-Layer Capacitor) with a maximum\nvoltage of 3.8V. The voltage coming from the solar cell in bright light\nmustn’t exceed the maximum voltage of the capacitor.\n\nTransistors:\nThe transistors can be standard NPN and PNP types with high enough gain.\nAs NPN, we use a BC547, maybe the most common NPN type in Europe.\nThe US equivalent would be the 2N3904, which should work fine here.\nAs PNP, we use the more exotic 2SB892, simply because of its elongated\nbody shape so it can be easily visually distinguished in the DIY kit.\nA BC557 or 2N3906 should work fine here.\n\nLEDs:\nWe use red LEDs with a low forward voltage (Vf) of about 1.8V. Green and\nyellow LEDs with a Vf up to about 2.2V might possibly work; LEDs with\nan even higher Vf (blue, white) will most probably not work.\nThe three LEDs are in parallel; one or two LEDs would flash a bit\nbrighter, more LEDs in parallel less bright.\n\nOther components:\nThe values of the other resistors and capacitors are carefully chosen\nand form a quite complex system. Values can be modified; e.g., changing\nthe capacitance of the 1µF capacitor will affect the length of the\nflashes, and changing the 33µF capacitor, the time between flashes.\nBut the components of the whole flasher circuit form a sensitive\nsystem, and changing the values of some components requires changing\nthe values of some other components to maintain this delicate balance.\nSmaller changes might work, but if some values are changed too much,\nthe whole circuit stops working."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 13.462 63.246 0)
